course: dictionary
lesson: 4856
title: Hypothermia: when the body gets too cold
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hypothermia is an illness. Your body keeps itself at a normal warm temperature, but when it gets much too cold, it cannot do this, and that is dangerous.
explain: Context helps you here. «Getting wet and cold when lost outdoors can lead to hypothermia, when the body gets dangerously cold.» The words after the comma explain the hard word, so hypothermia means the body getting dangerously cold.
explain: Watch for the signs: strong shivering, slurred speech, sleepiness and clumsy hands. Ben was shivering hard and mumbling after getting soaked on a frosty walk, so his body was getting dangerously cold.
explain: If someone shows these signs, get them out of the cold, take off their wet clothes, wrap them in blankets and call for help.
fact: Hypothermia means your body gets dangerously cold and cannot keep its normal warm temperature.
fact: Water pulls heat from your body about 25 times faster than air at the same temperature.
fact: Wet clothes keep pulling heat away from you, so getting soaked outdoors is a risk of hypothermia.
fact: Strong shivering, slurred speech, sleepiness and clumsy hands can all be signs of hypothermia.
fact: «Shivering a lot and feeling slow after playing in the cold can be the start of hypothermia.»
exercise: What does hypothermia mean? | A cold drink for a hot day | *A dangerous illness when your body gets too cold to keep its normal warm temperature | A type of warm bandage | A kind of sunburn from hot weather
exercise: Ben was shivering hard and mumbling after getting soaked. What might this show? | *His body is getting dangerously cold | He has a warm and cosy body | He is very happy about the rain | He has just finished his lunch
exercise: Which of these is a sign of hypothermia? | *Clumsy hands | Itchy feet | Laughing a lot | Singing loudly
test: «Water pulls heat from your body about 25 times faster than air at the same temperature.» What does this tell you about water? | Water is always hot | *Water takes heat from your body much faster than air | Water is warmer than air | Water makes you sleepy and nothing more
test: Someone outdoors is showing signs of hypothermia. What should you do first? | Wash them in cold water | Go for a long run to warm up | *Get them out of the cold and take off their wet clothes | Leave them alone to sleep
exam: Which words in «Getting wet and cold when lost outdoors can lead to hypothermia, when the body gets dangerously cold» explain what hypothermia means? | *the body gets dangerously cold | can lead to | lost outdoors | getting wet
